  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Find all pairs of primes $p,q$ for which $x^2+px-q=0$ has rational solutions.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(p,q)=(2,3)$ only.\n\nBy the Integer Root Theorem, the positive root must be $1$ or $q$. Only $1$ is possible, giving $q=p+1$.",
      "worked": "By the Integer Root Theorem, any rational root is an integer dividing $q$, so the possible roots are $\\pm1,\\pm q$. The roots have product $-q$, so one is positive. Write $P(x)=x^2+px-q$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(q)&=q^2+pq-q\\\\P(q)&=q(q+p-1)>0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus the positive root must be $1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1+p-q\\\\1+p-q&=0\\\\q&=p+1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nIf $p$ were an odd prime, $q=p+1$ would be an even integer greater than $2$, so it could not be prime. Therefore $p=2$ and $q=3$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+2x-3&=(x-1)(x+3).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe roots are $1$ and $-3$, both rational. Hence $(p,q)=(2,3)$ is the only pair.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
